.TH VM 1 "2026" "vm-manager" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vm \- create and manage QEMU virtual machines from prebuilt OS images
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vm
[\fB\-\-format\fR \fIFORMAT\fR]
.I COMMAND
[\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vm
runs virtual machines from prebuilt cloud images, with a command set modelled
on Docker. There is no daemon: each machine is a detached QEMU process, and
everything it owns lives in one directory under
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/vm/instances .
.PP
A machine is given its own writable disk over the stored image, its own SSH key
pair, and a cloud-init seed that installs that key for an account of its own.
A host port is forwarded to its SSH port whether or not one was published, so
.B vm ssh
works on a machine started with no arguments at all.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B images
List the images in the local catalogue.
.TP
.BI inspect " IMAGE"
Show where an image comes from and how its guest is reached.
.TP
.BI pull " IMAGE"
Fetch an image into the local store, verifying it against the catalogue digest.
.TP
.B update
Refresh the local catalogue from its remote source.
.TP
.BI run " IMAGE"
Create and start a machine. See
.B OPTIONS FOR RUN
below.
.TP
.BI start " NAME"
Start a machine that exists but is not running. It takes the same
.BR \-\-memory ", " \-\-cpus ", " \-\-publish ", " \-\-volume ", " \-\-user
and
.B \-\-disk\-size
options as
.BR run ,
each of which changes the machine for this start and every one after it;
what is not given is left as it was. A list of forwards or shares replaces the
one the machine had, and
.B \-\-no\-publish
or
.B \-\-no\-volume
leaves it with none. A running machine is not changed: these are read when a
machine starts, so it must be stopped first.
.TP
.BI ssh " NAME " [\fICOMMAND\fR]
Open a shell on a machine, or run a command in it. Wraps
.BR ssh (1),
so
.IR ~/.ssh/config ,
the agent and
.B ProxyCommand
all still apply.
.TP
.BI cp " FROM TO"
Copy files to or from a machine. One side is written as
.IR NAME:PATH .
.TP
.B ps
List machines.
.B \-\-all
includes those that are not running.
.TP
.BI stop " NAME"
Ask the guest to shut down, waiting
.B \-\-timeout
seconds before insisting.
.TP
.BI kill " NAME"
Stop a machine without telling the guest.
.TP
.BI logs " NAME"
Show what the machine has written to its console, across every boot since it
was created.
.B \-\-follow
writes new output as it arrives, until the machine stops, and
.BI \-n " COUNT"
shows only the last few lines.
.TP
.BI rmi " IMAGE"
Delete an image from the local store. An image the catalogue provides is listed
again as unfetched; one made here by
.B vm commit
has nowhere to be fetched from, so its catalogue entry goes with it. Machines
are built on their image, so one that is still in use is refused unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.TP
.BI commit " NAME IMAGE"
Save a machine's disk as a new image, named
.IR repository:tag .
A running guest is paused for the duration unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.TP
.BI rm " NAME"
Delete a machine and its disk.
.B \-\-force
removes one that is running.
.SH OPTIONS FOR RUN
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
Name the machine rather than take the one it invents.
.TP
.BI \-m ", " \-\-memory " AMOUNT"
Memory, in mebibytes unless suffixed with M or G. Default 2G.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpus " COUNT"
Processors. Default 2.
.TP
.BI \-p ", " \-\-publish " HOST:GUEST"
Forward a host port to a guest port. May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-v ", " \-\-volume " HOST:GUEST"
Share a host directory with the guest over virtiofs. May be repeated. A share
is mounted at every boot from the machine's seed and is not written to the
guest's
.IR /etc/fstab ,
so a machine started without it simply does not have it.
.TP
.BI \-\-user " NAME"
The account to create in the guest. Default
.BR vm .
.TP
.BI \-\-disk\-size " SIZE"
Grow the machine's disk to this size, such as 40G.
.TP
.BI \-\-pull " WHEN"
.BR always ", " missing " (the default) or " never .
.SH OUTPUT
Every command writes a table by default and machine-readable output on request:
.B \-\-format json
or
.BR "\-\-format yaml" .
In those formats the document is the only thing on standard output, with
progress and errors on standard error. Errors take the requested format too,
carrying a stable
.B kind
alongside the message.
.PP
.B vm logs \-\-follow
writes the console as it arrives, which no document format can do, so it is
refused there.
.PP
.B vm ssh
is the exception: it replaces itself with
.BR ssh (1),
so what reaches the terminal is the guest's output and not this tool's. A
script that needs to connect for itself should read
.B ssh_port
and
.B user
from
.BR "vm ps \-\-format json" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/vm/config.toml
Optional.
.B catalogue_url
and
.B catalogue_path
say where
.B vm update
fetches from;
.B auto_pull
turns on-demand fetching off.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/vm/images
The image store, addressed by digest.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/vm/catalogue
The catalogue as last fetched.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/vm/local
Catalogue entries for images made here by
.BR "vm commit" .
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/vm/instances
One directory per machine: its disk, key, console log and record. The console
log is appended to, so a machine that failed to boot and was started again
keeps the evidence.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vm
Monitor and share sockets, kept short because the kernel bounds the length of
a socket path.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VM_FORMAT
The default output format.
.TP
.B VM_CATALOGUE
Overrides the catalogue directory.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR scp (1),
.BR qemu\-system\-x86_64 (1),
.BR qemu\-img (1),
.BR virtiofsd (1)
